From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] linuxkpi: skb_header_cloned() must not claim every header is cloned

skb_header_cloned() returned true unconditionally (SKB_TODO).  Callers
treat that as "the header cannot be written in place" and respond by
calling pskb_expand_head(), which is itself a stub returning -ENXIO.

For a driver that checks it on the transmit path the combination is fatal:
brcmfmac's brcmf_netdev_start_xmit() tests

    if (skb_headroom(skb) < drvr->hdrlen || skb_header_cloned(skb))
            ret = pskb_expand_head(...);

so every single outgoing frame was dropped with -6 before ever reaching
the hardware, with no diagnostic beyond an incremented tx_dropped.

This KPI has no skb_clone() at all -- nothing ever produces two skbs
sharing one data buffer -- so false is the truthful answer, not merely the
convenient one.

pskb_expand_head() is left as it is; with this fixed, callers only reach
it when the headroom really is too small.

Signed-off-by: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/skbuff.h b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/skbuff.h
index 9e6f42cd4..68c66eded 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/skbuff.h
+++ b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/skbuff.h
@@ -1029,8 +1029,15 @@ static inline bool
 skb_header_cloned(struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
 	SKB_TRACE(skb);
-	SKB_TODO();
-	return (true);
+	/*
+	 * This KPI has no skb_clone(): nothing ever produces two skbs
+	 * sharing one data buffer, so a header is never cloned.  Returning
+	 * true (as this did) is not the safe answer -- callers react to it
+	 * by calling pskb_expand_head(), which is still a stub that fails
+	 * with -ENXIO.  brcmfmac's tx path does exactly that on every frame,
+	 * so every single packet was dropped before reaching the radio.
+	 */
+	return (false);
 }
 
 static inline uint8_t *
